1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,811
One prompt.

2
00:00:01,488 --> 00:00:02,570
Five hidden jobs.

3
00:00:03,237 --> 00:00:04,567
And an answer you can't check.

4
00:00:05,183 --> 00:00:07,610
That's what happens when you ask Claude to

5
00:00:07,610 --> 00:00:10,152
analyse a quarter of market data and propose

6
00:00:10,152 --> 00:00:11,654
strategies, all in one go.

7
00:00:12,254 --> 00:00:13,308
Here's where we're heading:

8
00:00:13,963 --> 00:00:16,667
the same request as five small steps, each one

9
00:00:16,667 --> 00:00:17,255
checkable.

10
00:00:17,849 --> 00:00:20,290
By the end of this lesson, you'll be able to

11
00:00:20,290 --> 00:00:22,842
break any big business request into a chain of

12
00:00:22,842 --> 00:00:25,395
steps that Claude gets right, and that you can

13
00:00:25,395 --> 00:00:25,783
verify.

14
00:00:26,383 --> 00:00:27,119
Chapter one.

15
00:00:27,836 --> 00:00:29,137
Spot the hidden jobs.

16
00:00:29,737 --> 00:00:31,539
Here's a prompt many of us have typed:

17
00:00:32,162 --> 00:00:35,180
analyse our Q3 market data and propose five

18
00:00:35,180 --> 00:00:35,952
strategies.

19
00:00:36,514 --> 00:00:38,845
It looks like one request, but it hides at

20
00:00:38,845 --> 00:00:39,733
least five jobs:

21
00:00:40,315 --> 00:00:42,798
read the data, spot the trends, explain the

22
00:00:42,798 --> 00:00:44,992
causes, generate ideas, and rank them.

23
00:00:45,574 --> 00:00:48,076
Asked in one breath, the model has to juggle

24
00:00:48,076 --> 00:00:49,213
all of them at once.

25
00:00:49,788 --> 00:00:52,108
The usual result is generic strategies,

26
00:00:52,108 --> 00:00:54,727
skipped steps, or numbers that were never in

27
00:00:54,727 --> 00:00:55,262
the data.

28
00:00:55,862 --> 00:00:58,093
Chain of thought means asking the model to

29
00:00:58,093 --> 00:01:00,376
write out its reasoning before it gives the

30
00:01:00,376 --> 00:01:00,748
answer.

31
00:01:01,341 --> 00:01:03,724
Each step is on the page, so later steps can

32
00:01:03,724 --> 00:01:06,052
build on earlier ones, and you can read the

33
00:01:06,052 --> 00:01:08,489
reasoning to see exactly where it went wrong.

34
00:01:09,059 --> 00:01:11,752
Same model, same data, but more of the work

35
00:01:11,752 --> 00:01:14,446
happens out loud, which usually means fewer

36
00:01:14,446 --> 00:01:16,325
errors on multi-step problems.

37
00:01:16,925 --> 00:01:19,133
Task decomposition works one level up.

38
00:01:19,714 --> 00:01:22,169
Instead of one prompt doing everything, you

39
00:01:22,169 --> 00:01:24,281
break the project into small, ordered

40
00:01:24,281 --> 00:01:24,852
sub-tasks.

41
00:01:25,421 --> 00:01:28,005
Each one has a clear input, a single job, and

42
00:01:28,005 --> 00:01:28,867
a clear output.

43
00:01:29,434 --> 00:01:31,934
Chain of thought improves the reasoning inside

44
00:01:31,934 --> 00:01:32,314
a step.

45
00:01:32,941 --> 00:01:35,520
Decomposition improves the structure between

46
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:35,872
steps.

47
00:01:36,424 --> 00:01:37,372
You'll want both.

48
00:01:37,972 --> 00:01:38,586
Takeaway:

49
00:01:39,327 --> 00:01:42,148
if a request hides several jobs, give each job

50
00:01:42,148 --> 00:01:42,945
its own step.

51
00:01:43,545 --> 00:01:44,318
Chapter two.

52
00:01:45,003 --> 00:01:46,576
Turn one prompt into five.

53
00:01:47,176 --> 00:01:48,408
Here's the before version:

54
00:01:49,053 --> 00:01:51,764
one prompt, the Q3 data pasted in, and a

55
00:01:51,764 --> 00:01:53,661
request for five strategies.

56
00:01:54,253 --> 00:01:55,253
You do get an answer.

57
00:01:55,908 --> 00:01:58,461
But you can't tell which numbers came from the

58
00:01:58,461 --> 00:02:01,014
data, why these five were chosen, or where the

59
00:02:01,014 --> 00:02:01,902
reasoning broke.

60
00:02:02,502 --> 00:02:03,712
Now the after version.

61
00:02:04,353 --> 00:02:04,976
Step one:

62
00:02:05,672 --> 00:02:08,011
extract the key metrics, and quote the source

63
00:02:08,011 --> 00:02:08,271
rows.

64
00:02:08,847 --> 00:02:09,530
Step two:

65
00:02:10,247 --> 00:02:11,719
find the three biggest trends.

66
00:02:12,292 --> 00:02:12,997
Step three:

67
00:02:13,676 --> 00:02:15,667
explain the likely cause of each trend.

68
00:02:16,284 --> 00:02:16,977
Step four:

69
00:02:17,685 --> 00:02:20,446
brainstorm ten strategy options tied to those

70
00:02:20,446 --> 00:02:20,876
causes.

71
00:02:21,444 --> 00:02:22,173
Step five:

72
00:02:22,842 --> 00:02:25,326
score them on impact and effort, and pick the

73
00:02:25,326 --> 00:02:25,878
best five.

74
00:02:26,456 --> 00:02:28,807
Every output becomes the next step's input.

75
00:02:29,407 --> 00:02:30,450
Phrasing matters.

76
00:02:31,101 --> 00:02:33,361
Plain, think step by step, helps a little.

77
00:02:33,980 --> 00:02:35,559
Better is to spell out the steps:

78
00:02:36,201 --> 00:02:38,337
first do this, then that, then the next thing.

79
00:02:39,031 --> 00:02:40,380
Best for review is structure.

80
00:02:41,049 --> 00:02:43,484
Ask Claude to put its reasoning inside

81
00:02:43,484 --> 00:02:46,112
thinking tags and the final result inside

82
00:02:46,112 --> 00:02:46,881
answer tags.

83
00:02:47,444 --> 00:02:49,765
Now you can read the work, and your code or a

84
00:02:49,765 --> 00:02:51,776
colleague can pull out just the answer.

85
00:02:52,376 --> 00:02:54,971
For bigger projects, run each step as its own

86
00:02:54,971 --> 00:02:56,989
prompt and pass the output forward.

87
00:02:57,603 --> 00:02:58,870
That's called prompt chaining.

88
00:02:59,501 --> 00:03:02,138
Between steps you can check the result, fix a

89
00:03:02,138 --> 00:03:04,365
mistake early, or rerun just one step.

90
00:03:04,931 --> 00:03:07,420
If step two found the wrong trends, you fix

91
00:03:07,420 --> 00:03:09,619
step two, not the whole strategy deck.

92
00:03:10,219 --> 00:03:10,833
Takeaway:

93
00:03:11,599 --> 00:03:13,878
make the reasoning visible, and check each

94
00:03:13,878 --> 00:03:15,832
output before the next step uses it.

95
00:03:16,432 --> 00:03:17,243
Chapter three.

96
00:03:17,936 --> 00:03:19,293
Decompose it yourself.

97
00:03:19,893 --> 00:03:20,981
Pause the video now.

98
00:03:21,618 --> 00:03:22,576
Take this task:

99
00:03:23,225 --> 00:03:25,368
plan the launch of a new product next quarter.

100
00:03:26,026 --> 00:03:27,560
Write four to six sub-tasks.

101
00:03:28,180 --> 00:03:30,737
For each one, note its input, its output, and

102
00:03:30,737 --> 00:03:32,840
how you'd check that output is right.

103
00:03:33,428 --> 00:03:35,702
Then write the prompt for step one, using

104
00:03:35,702 --> 00:03:37,422
explicit step-by-step phrasing.

105
00:03:38,016 --> 00:03:40,193
Give yourself five minutes, then press play.

106
00:03:40,793 --> 00:03:42,312
Here's one possible answer.

107
00:03:42,975 --> 00:03:44,819
Define the audience and the launch goal.

108
00:03:45,473 --> 00:03:46,619
Scan the competitors.

109
00:03:47,244 --> 00:03:49,280
Write the positioning and the key message.

110
00:03:49,849 --> 00:03:51,596
Choose channels and split the budget.

111
00:03:52,246 --> 00:03:54,346
Then build the timeline and list the risks.

112
00:03:54,903 --> 00:03:56,976
Yours may look different, and that's fine.

113
00:03:57,575 --> 00:03:58,189
Takeaway:

114
00:03:58,938 --> 00:04:01,754
every step gets one job, and an output you can

115
00:04:01,754 --> 00:04:02,121
check.

116
00:04:02,721 --> 00:04:03,512
Chapter four.

117
00:04:04,239 --> 00:04:05,432
Know when to use it.

118
00:04:06,033 --> 00:04:08,736
Decompose when the task has several stages,

119
00:04:08,736 --> 00:04:11,061
involves numbers, drives an important

120
00:04:11,061 --> 00:04:13,826
decision, or when someone needs to audit the

121
00:04:13,826 --> 00:04:14,455
reasoning.

122
00:04:15,038 --> 00:04:17,694
Keep it to one prompt for simple lookups and

123
00:04:17,694 --> 00:04:20,230
quick rewrites, where extra steps only add

124
00:04:20,230 --> 00:04:21,075
time and cost.

125
00:04:21,675 --> 00:04:22,289
Takeaway:

126
00:04:23,067 --> 00:04:25,639
decompose when the work has stages, numbers,

127
00:04:25,639 --> 00:04:26,223
or stakes.

128
00:04:26,834 --> 00:04:28,250
Otherwise, keep it simple.

129
00:04:28,850 --> 00:04:29,675
Let's recap.

130
00:04:30,372 --> 00:04:31,945
Big prompts hide many jobs.

131
00:04:32,545 --> 00:04:34,981
Chain of thought and explicit phrasing make

132
00:04:34,981 --> 00:04:36,001
the steps visible.

133
00:04:36,601 --> 00:04:39,437
Each step gets one job, a clear input, and an

134
00:04:39,437 --> 00:04:40,761
output you can check.

135
00:04:41,361 --> 00:04:43,455
And you decompose when the work is

136
00:04:43,455 --> 00:04:45,734
multi-stage, numeric, or high-stakes.
